      "name": "IsMemoryReadable",
      "signature": "bool IsMemoryReadable(void * pcBuffer, uint dwSize)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "bool",
      "comment": "Validates whether a memory region is readable without causing access violations.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Call Windows API IsBadReadPtr to test memory accessibility\n2. Return inverted result (1 if memory is bad, 0 if readable)\n3. Return true if readable (result == 0), false if memory access fails\n\nParameters:\n  pcBuffer: const void * - Memory address to validate\n  dwSize: uint - Number of bytes to check for readability\n\nReturns:\n  bool - true if entire region [pcBuffer, pcBuffer+dwSize) is readable without access violation\n         false if any byte in the region is inaccessible\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  NULL pointer or zero-length region: depends on IsBadReadPtr implementation\n  Guard pages: IsBadReadPtr may raise exceptions on guard pages (stack/heap)\n\nRelated Functions:\n  ProcessExceptionFrameRestoration - Caller context for exception frame validation\n  CppExceptionFilter - Caller context for C++ exception handling",
