      "name": "ValidateCodePointer",
      "signature": "bool ValidateCodePointer(void * pfnCodePtr)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "bool",
      "comment": "Wrapper that validates a function pointer by calling IsBadCodePtr and inverting the result.\n\nThis function checks if a given code pointer is valid and executable by leveraging the Win32 API IsBadCodePtr function, then returns the inverted boolean result. IsBadCodePtr returns 0 for valid code pointers and non-zero for invalid ones, so the wrapper inverts this to return true for valid pointers and false for invalid ones.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Call IsBadCodePtr(pfnCodePtr) - returns 0 if valid, non-zero if bad\n2. Test the return value in EAX: if EAX is 0 (valid), set ESI=1, else ESI=0\n3. Return ESI (true for valid, false for invalid)\n\nParameters:\npfnCodePtr: void* - Any function pointer to validate (can be NULL, will return false)\n\nReturns:\ntrue - if pfnCodePtr is valid and references executable code\nfalse - if pfnCodePtr is invalid, NULL, or non-executable\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- NULL pointers return false (IsBadCodePtr returns true for NULL)\n- Guard pages and non-executable memory cause false return\n- Used by exception frame and handler validation routines\n\nRelated Functions:\n- ProcessExceptionFrameRestoration - Validates exception handlers before invocation\n- ValidateExceptionHandlerStructure - Structural validation using this pointer check",
